sources read:
  samples/fixtures/token_breakdown.bash.jsonl

attribution coverage: 4 of 4 assistant turns were usage-bearing; 0 carried no recognised usage field
usage-field presence across usage-bearing turns (a field missing here is a silent 0, not a measured one):
  input_tokens: 4/4
  output_tokens: 4/4
  cache_creation_input_tokens: 4/4
  cache_read_input_tokens: 4/4
sidechain (subagent) turns: 0
  (0 means no sidechain turns in the transcripts PASSED, not that no subagent ran: some
   layouts write a subagent's turns to a separate transcript file. See --help-blind, mode 4b.)

TOTAL over 4 usage-bearing turns: in=4 out=100 cache_create=1000 cache_read=10000
cache_read : output overhead = 100.00

BY TOOL (turn-grain: a turn's usage is attributed to the SET of tools it called, not split per call):
  turns      out  cache_read cache_create  tools
      4      100       10000         1000  Bash

BY BASH COMMAND (Bash turns clustered by command's first token after stripping `cd ...&&` chains and `VAR=` prefixes; a heuristic, not a parse -- `git commit`/`git push` both cluster as `git`. Keys are a CLOSED vocabulary: a public command word, `(other)`, or `(unparsed)`):
  turns      out  cache_read cache_create  bash command cluster
      1       40        4000          400  (unparsed)
      2       30        3000          300  git
      1       30        3000          300  pytest

BY AGENT (same-session Task subagents separated from the main thread):
  turns      out  cache_read cache_create  agent
      4      100       10000         1000  main thread

TOP 5 TURNS BY cache_read (the turns re-reading the most cached context):
   cache_read      out  agent / tools
         4000       40  main thread / Bash
         3000       30  main thread / Bash
         2000       20  main thread / Bash
         1000       10  main thread / Bash

LOOP FLAGS
  Mechanical signals, computed from transcript STRUCTURE only -- turn boundaries and
  tool_use block counts -- never from judging what was said. THESE ARE FLAGS, NOT
  VERDICTS: a flagged span means an unusual amount of work by the threshold YOU set,
  which is a pointer to look. It is not a finding that the work was wasted, and no
  threshold here is a validated constant.

  real user turns (span boundaries): 0
  user entries NOT counted as operator input: none
    No isMeta, tool_result-only or engine-injected user entry was seen at all. For a
    real Claude Code transcript that is itself unusual -- tool results come back as user
    entries -- so check that this is the format you think it is.

  SIGNAL 1 -- tool calls per span between consecutive real user turns
    UNMEASURED: not one real user turn was found, so this transcript has no span
    boundaries at all. The whole file would read as ONE span and would exceed any
    threshold, so NOTHING is flagged here: a dramatic number about a session whose turns
    cannot be seen is worse than no number. See --help-blind, blind mode 6.

  SIGNAL 2 -- repeated / near-duplicate invocations: NOT IMPLEMENTED
      NOT IMPLEMENTED -- repeated/near-duplicate invocations. Published work rates this
      the most frequent of 14 multi-agent failure modes (MAST FM-1.3, step repetition,
      15.7% over 1600+ annotated traces), so it is the next signal to add, not one that
      was judged unimportant. It is deferred rather than approximated: matching on tool
      NAME alone would report a Bash-heavy session as one long repetition, and the
      command-level clustering that makes it meaningful is being built separately.
      Nothing partial is reported under its name.

  SIGNAL 3 -- consecutive assistant turns that called no tool
    longest streak anywhere: 0 turn(s)
    main thread: 4 turns, 0 with no tool call, in 0 streak(s), longest=0
    sidechains: 0 turns, 0 with no tool call, in 0 streak(s), longest=0
    threshold: NOT SET -> NOT COMPUTED
      No streak was tested. Same reason as signal 1 for shipping no default; set one
      with --flag-no-tool-streak N or ARI_FLAG_NO_TOOL_STREAK.
